# A name the body binds by its own code holds the value the body made there, in its frame, from the binding on (#237,
# #355), and a change in place makes it anew. A choice's value is what its arms make of it: what one arm makes anew of a
# value named before the choice, which the other arm keeps, is one value at the choice's moment, as it is of a value a step
# made (#366). `picked` binds an empty vector and pushes the level onto it where it is loud; `gathered` binds a vector of
# the first level, reading it there, and pushes the second where it is loud. In both the push takes the body's own vector,
# which stands in the route's frame, and the step after the choice takes the choice's value. `loud` is of no type of the
# code's, and the route's own parameters of no type hold none.

route levels.picked
choice picked/?1                                     # `if loud { .. }`
step picked/?1/push
step picked/list

line entry > picked call
line Level·level > picked value                      # the route's own parameter, from its start
line Level·both@picked/~both > picked/?1/push value  # `let mut both: Vec<&Level> = vec![]`, the body's own
line Level·level > picked/?1/push value              # `both.push(level)`
line picked/?1/push > Level·both@picked/?1 value     # made anew in one arm, kept in the other
line Level·both@picked/?1 > picked/list value        # `list(&both)`
line picked/list > Screen own

home Level·level picked
home Level·both@picked/~both picked
home Level·both@picked/?1 picked

side entry left
side Screen right

route levels.gathered
choice gathered/?1
step gathered/?1/push
step gathered/list

line entry > gathered call
line Level·first > gathered value                    # `let mut both = vec![first]`, read where it is bound
line Level·second > gathered value
line Level·both@gathered/~both > gathered/?1/push value
line Level·second > gathered/?1/push value           # `both.push(second)`
line gathered/?1/push > Level·both@gathered/?1 value
line Level·both@gathered/?1 > gathered/list value
line gathered/list > Screen own

home Level·first gathered
home Level·second gathered
home Level·both@gathered/~both gathered
home Level·both@gathered/?1 gathered

side entry left
side Screen right

# The same rule in a loop: the vector the body binds is the name's value for every step after the binding, in the loop as
# much as straight on, never the latest level the loop gives. A loop is a step: it takes what it goes over and gives each
# element under the name it binds. The push takes the body's vector and the element and makes the vector anew; the step
# after the loop takes what the push made.

route levels.looped
step looped/for level in levels
step looped/push
step looped/list

line entry > looped call
line Level·levels > looped value
line Level·levels > looped/for level in levels value
line looped/for level in levels > Level·level@looped/for level in levels value
line Level·every@looped/~every > looped/push value   # `let mut every: Vec<&Level> = vec![]`
line Level·level@looped/for level in levels > looped/push value
line looped/push > Level·every@looped/push value
line Level·every@looped/push > looped/list value
line looped/list > Screen own

home Level·levels looped
home Level·level@looped/for level in levels looped
home Level·every@looped/~every looped
home Level·every@looped/push looped

side entry left
side Screen right
